<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="practice-problems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exam 3 Practice Problems</title>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-limit-dne" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the following limit does not exist
        <md>
          \lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)}\frac{3xy}{2x^2+5y^2}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <md>f(x,y)=\frac{3xy}{2x^2+5y^2}</md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Method 1 (the limit along two paths).</alert> 
        <p>Along <m>y=0</m>:</p>
        <md>
          \lim_{x\to 0} f(x,0)=\lim_{x\to 0}\frac{0}{2x^2}=0
        </md>.
        Along <m>y=x</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>\lim_{x\to 0} f(x,x) \amp = \lim_{x\to 0}\frac{3x^2}{2x^2+5x^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x\to 0}\frac{3x^2}{7x^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{3}{7}</mrow>
        </md>.
        Since <m>0\neq\frac{3}{7}</m>, the limit does not exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Method 2 (the limit along all lines <m>y=mx</m>).</alert>
        <md>
          <mrow>\lim_{x\to 0} f(x,mx) \amp = \lim_{x\to 0}\frac{3mx^2}{2x^2+5m^2x^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \lim_{x\to 0}\frac{3m}{2+5m^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{3m}{2+5m^2}</mrow>
        </md>.
        This value depends on <m>m</m>, so the limit does not exist.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-classify-critical-points" workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the locations of any local maxima, local minima, and saddle points of
        <m>f(x,y)=x^3+y^3-3xy</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The first partial derivatives are
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = 3x^2-3y</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = 3y^2-3x</mrow>
        </md>.
        Setting <m>f_x(x,y)=0</m> gives <m>y=x^2</m>. Substituting into <m>f_y(x,y)=0</m> gives
        <md>
          x^4-x = x(x^3-1) = 0
        </md>,
        so <m>x=0</m> or <m>x=1</m>. The critical points are <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(1,1)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second partial derivatives are
        <md>
          <mrow>f_{xx}(x,y) \amp = 6x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xy}(x,y) \amp = -3</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{yy}(x,y) \amp = 6y</mrow>
        </md>,
        so
        <md>
          <mrow>D(x,y) \amp = (6x)(6y)-(-3)^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = 36xy-9</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>D(0,0)=36(0)(0)-9=-9\lt 0</m>, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>D(1,1)=36(1)(1)-9=27\gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}(1,1)=6\gt 0</m>, so <m>(1,1)</m> is a local minimum.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            There are no local maxima.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Alternative functions for the problem above (swap one into the statement if desired):
         f(x,y) = 2x^3 + 6xy + 3y^2         critical points (0,0) saddle; (1,-1) local min, f = -1
         f(x,y) = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy + 1       critical points (0,0) saddle; (1,1), (-1,-1) local min, f = -1
         f(x,y) = xy - x^2 y - x y^2        critical points (0,0), (1,0), (0,1) saddles; (1/3,1/3) local max, f = 1/27
  -->


  <exercise xml:id="ex-clairaut">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <md>
          f(x,y)=x\,e^{xy}+\frac{x}{1+y^2}
        </md>.
        Compute the mixed partial derivatives <m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m>,
        and verify that Clairaut's
        theorem holds.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Use the product rule and chain rule on the first term and the quotient rule on the second term:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = (1)\,e^{xy}+x\,(y\,e^{xy})+\frac{(1)(1+y^2)-x(0)}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = (1+xy)\,e^{xy}+\frac{1}{1+y^2}</mrow>
        </md>
        <md>
          <mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = x\,(x\,e^{xy})+\frac{(0)(1+y^2)-x(2y)}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = x^2e^{xy}-\frac{2xy}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
        </md>.
        Differentiating <m>f_x(x,y)</m> with respect to <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_{xy}(x,y) \amp = (x)\,e^{xy}+(1+xy)(x\,e^{xy})+\frac{(0)(1+y^2)-(1)(2y)}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = (2x+x^2y)\,e^{xy}-\frac{2y}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
        </md>.
        Differentiating <m>f_y(x,y)</m> with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_{yx}(x,y) \amp = 2x\,e^{xy}+x^2(y\,e^{xy})-\frac{2y}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = (2x+x^2y)\,e^{xy}-\frac{2y}{(1+y^2)^2}</mrow>
        </md>.
        Therefore <m>f_{xy}(x,y)=f_{yx}(x,y)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-isotherms">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The map below shows the level curves of the temperature function <m>T(x,y)</m> in degrees Fahrenheit.
        (As usual, we assume the positive <m>x</m>-axis points east and the positive <m>y</m>-axis points north.)
        The points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>\ldots</m>, <m>H</m> are marked on the map, with coordinates
        <m>A=(x_A,y_A)</m>, <m>B=(x_B,y_B)</m>, and so on.
      </p>
      <image source="us-isotherms.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Temperature contour map of the contiguous United States with labeled points A through H and direction vectors</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A map of the contiguous United States with isotherms (level curves of temperature) labeled from <m>55</m> degrees in the north to <m>90</m> degrees along the Texas Gulf Coast.
            Temperatures generally increase from north to south.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>A</m> is near the Gulf Coast of Louisiana, between the <m>80</m> and <m>85</m> degree curves. Eight vectors labeled <m>\bvec{a}</m> through <m>\bvec{h}</m> radiate from <m>A</m> in the compass directions north, northeast, east, southeast, south, southwest, west, and northwest.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>B</m> is in Montana, on the northern edge of a warm closed region, near the <m>65</m> degree curve.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>C</m> is in the Texas Panhandle, between the <m>75</m> degree curve to the north and the <m>80</m> degree curve to the south.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>D</m> is in Kansas, inside a cooler tongue bounded by the <m>70</m> degree curve. A unit vector <m>\bvec{u}</m> at <m>D</m> points southwest, toward the <m>70</m> degree curve.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>E</m> is in Wyoming, at the center of a small warm region of closed curves.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>F</m> is in Virginia and West Virginia, at the center of a cool closed <m>60</m> degree curve.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>G</m> is in Utah, between the warm closed regions around <m>E</m> (to the northeast) and <m>H</m> (to the southwest), with cooler temperatures to the west and east.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>H</m> is in southern Nevada, at the center of a warm bullseye of tightly spaced closed curves (<m>75</m>, <m>80</m>, <m>85</m>, and warmer).</p></li>
          </ul>
        </description>
      </image>
    </introduction>

    <task label="iso-1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which of the vectors <m>\bvec{a}</m>, <m>\bvec{b}</m>, <m>\ldots</m>, <m>\bvec{h}</m> points in the direction of <m>\grad T(x_A,y_A)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{a}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{b}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{c}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{d}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{e}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{f}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{g}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Vector <m>\bvec{h}</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>


    <task label="iso-2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which is greater, <m>|\grad T(x_A,y_A)|</m> or <m>|\grad T(x_B,y_B)|</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
