        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Point <m>F</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Point <m>G</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Point <m>H</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>None of these</p></statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task label="iso-6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which point is a saddle point of <m>T</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Point <m>C</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Point <m>E</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Point <m>F</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Point <m>G</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Point <m>H</m></p></statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>None of these</p></statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-plane-linearization">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x,y)=\sqrt{x^2+3y}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the equation of the tangent plane to the graph <m>z=f(x,y)</m> at
          the point <m>(1,1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have <m>f(1,1)=\sqrt{1+3}=2</m>. So, <m>(1,1,2)</m> is a point on the plane. The partial derivatives are
          <md>
            <mrow>f_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+3y}}</mrow>
            <mrow>f_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{3}{2\sqrt{x^2+3y}}</mrow>
          </md>.
          So, <m>f_x(1,1)=\frac{1}{2}</m> and <m>f_y(1,1)=\frac{3}{4}</m>, and hence 
                    <md>
            \bvec{n}=\langle f_x(1,1),\,f_y(1,1),\,-1\rangle=\left\langle \tfrac{1}{2},\,\tfrac{3}{4},\,-1\right\rangle
          </md> is a normal vector to the tangent plane.
          So, the equation of the tangent plane is
          <md>
            \tfrac{1}{2}(x-1)+\tfrac{3}{4}(y-1)-(z-2)=0.
            </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the linearization <m>L(x,y)</m> of <m>f</m> at the point
          <m>(1,1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The linearization is the function whose graph is the tangent plane:
          <md>
            L(x,y)=2+\tfrac{1}{2}(x-1)+\tfrac{3}{4}(y-1)
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your answer to part (b) to approximate <m>f(1.04,\,0.96)</m>. Then use a calculator to find the actual value, and compare.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>f(1.04,\,0.96) \approx L(1.04,\,0.96) \amp = 2+\tfrac{1}{2}(0.04)+\tfrac{3}{4}(-0.04)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = 2+0.02-0.03</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = 1.99</mrow>
          </md>.
          The actual value is <m>f(1.04,\,0.96)=\sqrt{3.9616}\approx 1.9904</m>, so the approximation is off by only about <m>0.0004</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hiking">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        You are hiking on a hill whose elevation (in meters) is
        <md>h(x,y)=1200-0.005x^2-0.01y^2</md> where <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are
        measured in meters, the positive <m>x</m>-axis points east, and the
        positive <m>y</m>-axis points north.
        You are standing at the point <m>(60,20)</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you walk northeast, are you ascending or descending?
          At what rate?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The gradient is <md>\grad h(x,y)=\langle -0.01x,\,-0.02y\rangle</md>. So,
          <md>\grad h(60,20)=\langle -0.6,\,-0.4\rangle</md>.
          Northeast is the direction of the unit vector <m>\bvec{u}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\langle 1,1\rangle</m>, so
          <md>
            <mrow>D_{\bvec{u}}h(60,20) \amp = \langle -0.6,\,-0.4\rangle\cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\langle 1,1\rangle</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} = -\tfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</mrow>
          </md>.
          Since the directional derivative is negative, you are descending, at a rate of <m>\tfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\approx 0.71</m> meters of elevation per meter walked.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In what direction should you walk to climb most steeply? (You do not have to give compass directions; a vector is sufficient.)
          What is the slope in that direction?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          You climb most steeply in the direction of <m>\grad h(60,20)=\langle -0.6,\,-0.4\rangle</m> (roughly southwest, but more west than south). The slope in this direction is
          <md>
            |\grad h(60,20)|=\sqrt{(-0.6)^2+(-0.4)^2}=\sqrt{0.52}\approx 0.72
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In what direction(s) should you walk to stay at the same elevation?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To stay at the same elevation, walk in a direction <m>\bvec{u}</m> with <m>D_{\bvec{u}}h(60,20)=\grad h(60,20)\cdot\bvec{u}=0</m>, that is, perpendicular to <m>\langle -0.6,-0.4\rangle</m>.
          Two such vectors are
          <md>
            \bvec{v}=\langle 0.4,-0.6\rangle \quad\text{and}\quad \bvec{w}=\langle -0.4,0.6\rangle
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="ex-box-volume" workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A rectangular box without a lid is to be made from <m>12\text{ m}^2</m>
        of cardboard.
        Find the maximum volume of such a box.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let the base be <m>x</m> by <m>y</m> and the height be <m>z</m> (all in meters). With no lid, the amount of cardboard is
        <m>xy+2xz+2yz=12</m>, so <m>z=\frac{12-xy}{2(x+y)}</m>. The volume is
        <md>
          V(x,y)=xyz=\frac{12xy-x^2y^2}{2(x+y)}
        </md>.
        Using the quotient rule and simplifying,
        <md>
          <mrow>V_x(x,y) \amp = \frac{(12y-2xy^2)\cdot 2(x+y)-(12xy-x^2y^2)\cdot 2}{4(x+y)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{y^2(12-2xy-x^2)}{2(x+y)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>V_y(x,y) \amp = \frac{(12x-2x^2y)\cdot 2(x+y)-(12xy-x^2y^2)\cdot 2}{4(x+y)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{x^2(12-2xy-y^2)}{2(x+y)^2}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Setting both equal to zero gives
        <md>
          <mrow>y^2(12-2xy-x^2) \amp = 0</mrow>
          <mrow>x^2(12-2xy-y^2) \amp = 0</mrow>
        </md>.
        Because <m>x,y\gt 0</m>, the solutions <m>x=0</m> and <m>y=0</m> are not in the domain, so
        <md>
          <mrow>12-2xy-x^2 \amp = 0</mrow>
          <mrow>12-2xy-y^2 \amp = 0</mrow>
        </md>.
        Subtracting the equations, we obtain <m>x^2=y^2</m>, and because <m>x,y\gt 0</m>, <m>x=y</m>. Then <m>3x^2=12</m>, so <m>x=y=2</m> and <m>z=\frac{12-4}{8}=1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The volume tends to <m>0</m> at the edges of the allowed region (as <m>x</m> or <m>y</m> approaches <m>0</m>, or <m>xy</m> approaches <m>12</m>), so this single critical point gives the maximum. The maximum volume is
        <m>V=2\cdot 2\cdot 1=4\text{ m}^3</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ex-second-derivative-test">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(1,2)</m> is a critical point of a function <m>f</m> with
        continuous second partial derivatives.
        In each case, what can you say about <m>f</m> at <m>(1,2)</m>?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task label="sdt-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f_{xx}(1,2)=4, \quad f_{yy}(1,2)=5, \quad f_{xy}(1,2)=3</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Local maximum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p>Local minimum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Correct. <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2=20-9=11\gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}(1,2)=4\gt 0</m>, so <m>f</m> has a local minimum at <m>(1,2)</m>.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Saddle point</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>The test is inconclusive</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task label="sdt-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f_{xx}(1,2)=4, \quad f_{yy}(1,2)=5, \quad f_{xy}(1,2)=6</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Local maximum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Local minimum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p>Saddle point</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Correct. <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2=20-36=-16\lt 0</m>, so <m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(1,2)</m>.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>The test is inconclusive</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task label="sdt-c">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f_{xx}(1,2)=-2, \quad f_{yy}(1,2)=-3, \quad f_{xy}(1,2)=1</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p>Local maximum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Correct. <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2=6-1=5\gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}(1,2)=-2\lt 0</m>, so <m>f</m> has a local maximum at <m>(1,2)</m>.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Local minimum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Saddle point</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>The test is inconclusive</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task label="sdt-d">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f_{xx}(1,2)=2, \quad f_{yy}(1,2)=8, \quad f_{xy}(1,2)=4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Local maximum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Local minimum</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice>
            <statement><p>Saddle point</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Incorrect. Compute <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2</m> and check its sign (and, if needed, the sign of <m>f_{xx}</m>).</p></feedback>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement><p>The test is inconclusive</p></statement>
            <feedback><p>Correct. <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2=16-16=0</m>, so the Second Derivatives Test gives no information.</p></feedback>
          </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>
  </exercise>





  <exercise xml:id="ex-true-false">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Determine whether each statement is true or false.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task label="tf-a">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>
          If <m>f(x,y)\to L</m> as <m>(x,y)\to(0,0)</m> along the lines <m>x=0</m> and <m>y=0</m>, then <md>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)}f(x,y)=L</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. Checking two paths is not enough. For <m>f(x,y)=\frac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>, the limit along both axes is <m>0</m>, but along <m>y=x</m> the limit is <m>\tfrac12</m>, so the limit does not exist.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-b">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>
          <m>D_{\bvec{u}}f(a,b)</m> is a vector.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. <m>D_{\bvec{u}}f(a,b)=\grad f(a,b)\cdot\bvec{u}</m> is a dot product, so it is a scalar (a rate of change), not a vector.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-c">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>
          <m>\grad f(a,b)</m> is orthogonal to the level curve of <m>f</m>
          through <m>(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          True. If <m>\bvec{r}(t)</m> parametrizes the level curve <m>f(x,y)=k</m>, differentiating <m>f(\bvec{r}(t))=k</m> gives <m>\grad f\cdot\bvec{r}\,'(t)=0</m>, so the gradient is orthogonal to the curve (when <m>\grad f(a,b)\neq\bvec{0}</m>).
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-d">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>
          The maximum value of <m>D_{\bvec{u}}f(a,b)</m> over all unit vectors
          <m>\bvec{u}</m> is <m>|\grad f(a,b)|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          True. <m>D_{\bvec{u}}f(a,b)=|\grad f(a,b)|\cos\theta</m>, which is largest when <m>\theta=0</m>, that is, when <m>\bvec{u}</m> points in the direction of <m>\grad f(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-e">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>
          For any unit vector <m>\bvec{u}</m>,
          <m>D_{-\bvec{u}}f(a,b)=-D_{\bvec{u}}f(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          True. <m>D_{-\bvec{u}}f(a,b)=\grad f(a,b)\cdot(-\bvec{u})=-\grad f(a,b)\cdot\bvec{u}=-D_{\bvec{u}}f(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-f">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>
          If <m>\grad f(a,b)=\bvec{0}</m>, then <m>f</m> has a local maximum or
          a local minimum at <m>(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. A critical point can be a saddle point. For <m>f(x,y)=x^2-y^2</m>, <m>\grad f(0,0)=\bvec{0}</m>, but <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-g">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>
          If <m>(a,b)</m> is a critical point of <m>f</m>,
          <m>f_{xx}(a,b)&gt;0</m>, and <m>f_{yy}(a,b)&gt;0</m>, then <m>f</m>
          has a local minimum at <m>(a,b)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          False. The test also requires <m>D=f_{xx}f_{yy}-f_{xy}^2&gt;0</m>. For <m>f(x,y)=x^2+4xy+y^2</m>, <m>f_{xx}=f_{yy}=2&gt;0</m>, but <m>D=4-16&lt;0</m>, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>

    <task label="tf-h">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> is differentiable and has a local maximum at <m>(a,b)</m>,
          then <m>\grad f(a,b)=\bvec{0}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>
          True. If <m>f</m> has a local maximum at <m>(a,b)</m>, then <m>g(x)=f(x,b)</m> and <m>h(y)=f(a,y)</m> have local maxima at <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, so <m>f_x(a,b)=f_y(a,b)=0</m>.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>